solar-system: a parallax set for the Neo launcher, made from NASA images (2026-11-12)

The planets, the Moon and the Sun are NASA photographs and visualizations, all
from NASA's own sites. The star field, the glows, the dust band, the orbit
lines and the asteroid belt are original procedural art drawn by
build_solar_system.py (the generator, kept beside this set in
neo-assets/public/scripts). No ESA, ESO, JAXA, Hubble or individually
credited image is used.

Layers, back to front (depth in parallax.json):
  layer0.jpg  -0.60  star field, glows, dust band (procedural)
  layer1.png  -0.25  orbit lines, asteroid belt (procedural); the Sun
  layer2.png   0.10  Earth, Moon, Mars
  layer3.png   0.55  Saturn
  layer4.png   1.00  Jupiter
wallpaper.jpg is the set flattened at rest (1080x2280); preview.jpg is 512x512.

Edits made to every image: cut out of its black background with an
antialiased edge, scaled, and shaded toward the Sun. Earth and Mars got a
thin atmosphere glow. The Saturn image is mirrored left to right, so that
its lit side faces the Sun. The Sun image is recoloured from SDO's 304 A
false colour to yellow-white.

Sources

