6. Jupiter: "Cassini Jupiter Portrait" (Cassini, Dec 2000)
   PIA04866 (NASA JPL Photojournal)
   https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/cassini-jupiter-portrait/
   https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia04/pia04866/PIA04866.jpg
   (the same image is NASA ID PIA04866 on images.nasa.gov; the Photojournal
   copy is the larger one and is the one used)
   Credit: NASA/JPL/Space Science Institute

Why these are public domain

Works made by NASA (a US federal agency) are not under copyright in the
United States (17 U.S.C. 105). NASA's media usage guidelines
(https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/) say NASA
content, including images, is generally not subject to copyright in the
US and may be used for educational or informational purposes, reproduced
and published without explicit permission. JPL's image use policy
(https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/jpl-image-use-policy) says JPL images, including
the Photojournal's, may be used for any purpose without prior permission
unless a copyright is noted on the image; none of PIA00407, PIA04866 or
PIA06193 notes one. The Space Science Institute led the Cassini imaging team
under NASA contract; its PIA images are released by NASA/JPL under that
policy. USGS (Viking mosaics) is a US federal agency too. The two Goddard
items and the Apollo 17 photograph name only NASA in their credits.

The guidelines ask for the credit lines above (attribution is courteous,
not a licence condition), and that NASA's name, logos and insignia not be
used to suggest endorsement. This set uses none of them.

The procedural parts (stars, glows, band, orbits, belt) are original work
drawn by build_solar_system.py for this set; they contain no third-party
art.
